  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1157 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nDry and very warm conditions will prevail through Friday,\nunderneath broad upper level ridging. While daytime highs average\n10 to 15 degrees above normal, the relatively dry air will cool\nefficiently at night. This dry air will also result in continued\nelevated wildland fire danger, which may peak on Friday, as winds\nand subsidence around the remnants of tropical system Isaias\napproach.\n\nRegarding TD9 / Isaias, intensification to tropical storm status\nis expected later today. This system is currently forecast to\nintensify to hurricane strength over the central Gulf Thursday\nevening, with winds of 85 knots forecast at landfall Friday\nevening. Current NHC forecasts show most likely landfall to be\nnear the MS/AL border. Isaias may quickly weaken post-landfall,\nas it encounters shear. The remnant low center is currently\nforecast to track over far northeast MS Saturday afternoon, then\naccelerate through the TN River Valley TN Saturday evening.\n\nA weak low over the TX coastal plain will aid Isaias' relatively\nquick northward track post-landfall. The fast forward motion will\nlimit rainfall potential, which should average 1-3 inches east of\nthe MS River, with up to 4 inches locally near the TN River.\nWinds from Isaias will likely reach Wind Advisory criteria east\nof the MS River, if current track and intensity forecasts pan\nout.\n\nAn upper level ridge over TX at the start of the upcoming\nworkweek will amplify as it lifts into the lower and middle MS\nRiver Valley by the middle of next week. Daytime highs in the 80s\nappear likely to continue into mid-October.",
